<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!-- THE PROMISE: nothing that identifies you, your address or your vehicle leaves this device.
         Not a full VIN, not a parcel number, not a lender, not a balance, not a plate.

         Maintenance used to keep that by declaring no permissions at all. The permission count was
         the means, never the promise, and the promise is the part that must not move. So the test
         for anything added here — Health's test, and for the same reason — is not "does it touch the
         network". It is: COULD THIS REQUEST TELL ANYONE SOMETHING ABOUT THIS HOUSEHOLD?

             "What is a 2018 Jeep Wrangler Unlimited Sport 3.6 4x4?"
                 → a question about a PRODUCT. Anyone could type it into a search engine.
             "Which truck is parked outside this address?"
                 → a question about a HOUSEHOLD. It never leaves.

         INTERNET — two lookups ask the first kind of question, and neither can ask the second.

           * VIN decode (`data/net/VehicleLookupClient.decode`) sends the first ELEVEN characters of
             a VIN with the rest wildcarded — `1C4HJXDG5JW******`. Those eleven describe the model;
             the six dropped are the serial, the part on your title and the part a history service
             is keyed on. They are dropped because they identify your vehicle AND because vPIC
             returns an identical answer without them. The rule is a tested function
             (`logic/Vin.decodeQuery`), not a habit somebody has to remember.

           * Recall check (`…recalls`) sends a make, a model and a year. No VIN at all. The answer is
             the same for every 2018 Wrangler in the country.

         Both run only because somebody pressed a button — nothing here polls, syncs or wakes up —
         and both answers are stored locally so the same question isn't asked twice. Everything else
         in this app (the mortgage, the parcel number, the service history, the odometer) has no code
         path to the network at all. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!-- Maintenance is a library module: no app-level attributes here (name/icon/theme/label live
         on the :app host). Only Maintenance's own components are declared for the manifest merger. -->
    <application>

        <!-- Not a launcher: the Operations Sandbox home opens this explicitly. -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Maintenance"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize" />

    </application>

</manifest>
